<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lu'lu' al-Kabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1009">1009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lu'lu' al-Kabir was a slave commander and chamberlain of the Hamdanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> who became the real power behind them. In 1004 he deposed the last Hamdanid heirs and ruled Aleppo in his own name, balancing between the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and accepting Fatimid recognition. On his death in 1009 his son Mansur succeeded him, but the regime lasted only until 1016.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His full name was Abu Muhammad Lu'lu', surnamed al-Kabir, the Elder, and al-Jarrahi al-Sayfi. No source records his early life, but these names suggest that he first served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrahids">Jarrahids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> before entering the service of the Hamdanid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>, under whom he is attested on an expedition against Mopsuestia in 965. His name, meaning pearl, was typical of the pet-names given to slave-soldiers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilman">ghilman</a>. The historian Fukuzo Amabe argues that he was rather a protege of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> of Sayf al-Dawla named Hajraj.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>'s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sad-al-dawla">Sa'd al-Dawla</a> he rose to be chamberlain (hajib). On his death-bed in 991 Sa'd al-Dawla entrusted him with his son Abu'l-Fada'il, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-dawla">Sa'id al-Dawla</a>. Lu'lu' secured the boy's succession, saved him when the adventurer Bakjur tried to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and married his own daughter to the young emir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rivals who resented his power went over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, who renewed their attacks. In 992 the Fatimid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> sent the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Manjutakin, who defeated the Byzantine doux of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, Michael Bourtzes, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> until lack of supplies forced him back in 993. In 994 he returned, beat Bourtzes again at the Battle of the Orontes and blockaded the city for eleven months. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-dawla">Sa'id al-Dawla</a> proposed surrender, Lu'lu''s determination kept the defenders holding out until Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in April 995 with 13,000 men, and Manjutakin burned his camp and withdrew.</p><p>Basil released the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> from its annual tribute, but in 995 Lu'lu' also acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, and Fatimid influence grew. A Byzantine garrison was later placed at Shayzar, and a treaty of 1001 brought a ten-year truce.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-dawla">Sa'id al-Dawla</a> died in January 1002, poisoned at Lu'lu''s behest according to a tradition recorded by Ibn al-Adim, Lu'lu' and his son Mansur took direct power, at first as guardians of the emir's sons Abu'l-Hasan Ali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sad-al-dawla">Abu'l-Ma'ali Sharif</a>, whom he later exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He was remembered for wisdom and justice: he recognised Fatimid suzerainty yet kept paying tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and imprisoned the adventurer al-Asfar, who wished to wage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the empire. The historian Marius Canard saw in him a forerunner of what the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of Egypt later became on a larger scale.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lu'lu' al-Kabir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Real power passed to the chamberlain <b>Lu'lu'</b>, who deposed the last Hamdanids in 1004, beginning Lu'lu'id Aleppo.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lu'lu' al-Kabir</b> was a slave commander and chamberlain of the Hamdanids of Aleppo who had long been the real power behind the young emirs.</blockquote><a href="/state/luluid-aleppo">Lu'lu'id Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=999" aria-label="Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5486 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Lu'lu' al-Kabir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=999">What was here in 999 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
